# AIRA local Docker Compose configuration.
# Copy to `.env` (git-ignored) and adjust for your machine:  cp .env.example .env
# These are LOCAL DEV values only — real secrets live in HashiCorp Vault, never here.
#
# ==============================================================================================
# WHAT DO YOU WANT TO START?
# ==============================================================================================
#
#   make up          infrastructure only — Postgres, Keycloak, Kafka, Redis, Vault, collector,
#                    Grafana. Nothing of AIRA itself runs. This is what `make run-gateway` and
#                    the development shape sit on top of.
#   make up-core     the same, without the observability backend.
#   make up-apps     infrastructure + the AIRA processes in containers. No demo data.
#   make up-full     everything, demo provisioning included.
#   make showcase    the guided demo: a local model is pulled, roles/budgets are seeded and real
#                    traffic is generated. Takes a few minutes on the first run.
#   make up-integrated  the AIRA processes alone, against Postgres, Keycloak, Kafka and Vault you
#                    provide — every address required (docs/deployment/integrated.md §5a).
#
# A second telemetry destination, for trying a SIEM alongside, is two variables on any of these —
# see "TWO CHANNELS" below.
#
# The four shapes an installation takes are documented one page each — read the one you are in:
#   docs/deployment/showcase.md · standalone.md · dev.md · integrated.md
#
# ==============================================================================================
# THE SIX SWITCHES THAT CHANGE WHAT YOU GET
# ==============================================================================================
#
# Everything else has a default that is the right answer. These do not:
#
#   AIRA_ENVIRONMENT         `local` waives every hardening check — seven kinds on the gateway, ten
#                            on Management. On anything else (no other value is special, `demo`
#                            included) a dev secret, DEBUG, a wildcard ALLOWED_HOSTS, PLAINTEXT
#                            Kafka or OIDC without an audience refuses to boot.
#   AIRA_DEMO_MODE           seeds a demo API key and lets `seed_demo` run — and waives **six
#                            named** checks on the gateway (`WAIVED_BY_A_DEMO`) and **none** on
#                            Management, so outside `local` the gateway starts and the control
#                            plane still refuses. It does *not* waive AIRA_AUTH_REQUIRED or
#                            AIRA_REQUIRE_USE_CASE: a demo with those off is not this product.
#   AIRA_AUTH_REQUIRED       `false` serves every route to anyone who can reach the port.
#   AIRA_OIDC_ENABLED        whether the gateway verifies the console's Keycloak token.
#   AIRA_OTEL_ENABLED        whether the applications export anything at all. Off, the whole
#                            observability stack is healthy and receives nothing.
#   AIRA_DEBUG_INTEGRATIONS  one log line per call to an external system, while you wire one up.
#
# Four ready-made combinations:
#
#   # 1. Look at it (make up-full / make showcase)
#   AIRA_ENVIRONMENT=local   AIRA_DEMO_MODE=true   AIRA_AUTH_REQUIRED=true
#   AIRA_OIDC_ENABLED=true   AIRA_OTEL_ENABLED=true
#
#   # 2. Develop against it (make up + the run-* targets)
#   AIRA_ENVIRONMENT=local   AIRA_DEMO_MODE=true   AIRA_LOG_JSON=false
#   AIRA_OIDC_ENABLED=true   AIRA_OTEL_ENABLED=false
#
#   # 3. Wire it to your own infrastructure, one system at a time.
#   #    **Stay on `local`** for this. `AIRA_DEMO_MODE` waives six checks on the gateway and
#   #    NONE on Management, so any other environment with it gives you a stack where the
#   #    gateway starts and the control plane refuses — see §8 of docs/CONFIGURATION.md. The
#   #    point of this phase is that nothing blocks you while you attach Keycloak, then Kafka,
#   #    then Vault; the debug channel is what tells you whether each one answered.
#   AIRA_ENVIRONMENT=local   AIRA_DEBUG_INTEGRATIONS=all   AIRA_OTEL_ENABLED=true
#   AIRA_OIDC_ENABLED=true   AIRA_OIDC_ISSUER=https://keycloak.internal/realms/aira
#   #    Then move to combination 4 and let `make config-check` list what is still missing.
#
#   # 4. Production
#   AIRA_ENVIRONMENT=production   AIRA_DEMO_MODE=false   AIRA_AUTH_REQUIRED=true
#   AIRA_OIDC_ENABLED=true        AIRA_OIDC_AUDIENCE=aira-gateway
#   AIRA_DEBUG=false              AIRA_ALLOWED_HOSTS=aira.example.com
#   AIRA_PRIVACY_CONTROLLER="Example GmbH, …"
#   AIRA_KAFKA_SECURITY_PROTOCOL=SASL_SSL
#   VAULT_ADDR=https://vault.internal:8200   VAULT_ROLE_ID=<from make vault-init>
#   VAULT_SECRET_ID_FILE=/run/secrets/vault-secret-id
#   AIRA_OTEL_ENABLED=true        AIRA_DEBUG_INTEGRATIONS=
#   # and every credential out of this file and into Vault. **VAULT_ADDR alone is not Vault**:
#   # without a role id (or a token) nothing is read from it and every secret comes from this
#   # file, silently.
#
# ==============================================================================================
# REACHING THIS STACK FROM ANOTHER MACHINE  —  three things, and they must agree
# ==============================================================================================
#
# The defaults below are for a browser **on the Docker host**. Open the console from anywhere else
# and you get `server could not be reached` — but at one of several different walls, and each looks
# like the same message. In order:
#
#   1. AIRA_BIND_HOST / AIRA_BIND_HOST6   Nothing is listening on a reachable interface.
#      127.0.0.1 / ::1 by default, because this stack publishes Postgres, Redis, Kafka and a
#      dev-mode Vault whose credentials are printed in this very file. Set BOTH — Docker opens
#      one socket per published entry, so `0.0.0.0` alone leaves every IPv6 caller reaching a
#      machine with nothing listening, which arrives as a **reset** and sends you to the wrong end.
#
#        AIRA_BIND_HOST=0.0.0.0
#        AIRA_BIND_HOST6=::
#
#   2. AIRA_OIDC_ISSUER   The console loads, and the login goes nowhere.
#      Its default is `http://localhost:8080/realms/aira`, and the console's container writes that
#      string into `runtime-config.js` at every start and hands it to **the browser** — which
#      resolves `localhost` to the machine the browser is on. It is also what both planes check
#      `iss` against, so the two cannot differ.
#
#        AIRA_OIDC_ISSUER=http://aira.intern.example:8080/realms/aira
#
#      The same variable moves the console's content policy: its `connect-src` is derived from the
#      issuer's origin, so the token request is allowed wherever Keycloak went. Set
#      AIRA_CSP_CONNECT_SRC only for an origin that derivation cannot guess — and name the issuer's
#      origin in it as well, because a value replaces the derivation and does not follow the issuer:
#
#        AIRA_CSP_CONNECT_SRC='self' http://aira.intern.example:8080 wss://events.example
#
#   3. AIRA_CONSOLE_HOST   Keycloak refuses the redirect.
#      The realm pins its redirect URIs — a wildcard on a public client lets an attacker capture
#      the authorization code (`ADR-0007`) — so the host you actually type has to be one of them.
#      Defaults to `localhost`, and `localhost`/`127.0.0.1` stay valid whatever you set.
#
#        AIRA_CONSOLE_HOST=aira.intern.example
#
#      **Read by the realm import only**, which runs when the realm does not yet exist. On a stack
#      that already has one: `docker compose down -v keycloak` first, or add the URI in Keycloak's
#      own admin console.
#
# All three, together, for a stack at `aira.intern.example`:
#
#   AIRA_BIND_HOST=0.0.0.0
#   AIRA_BIND_HOST6=::
#   AIRA_CONSOLE_HOST=aira.intern.example
#   AIRA_OIDC_ISSUER=http://aira.intern.example:8080/realms/aira
#
# `make showcase` and every `make` target still print `http://localhost:…`: those addresses are
# for the machine running `make`, which is the Docker host. That is correct and is not what the
# browser was given.
#
# ==============================================================================================
#
# **Before you deploy any of them**, ask both planes' own start-up checks what they would refuse:
#
#   make config-check CONFIG=config/integrated.example.yaml
#
# It builds the settings from the rendered file alone and asks each plane's `unsafe_settings`. It
# builds no upstream, so a model list an adapter cannot read, a region outside the allowed list or
# a missing Foundry key is still first met at start (docs/CONFIGURATION.md §8). A file with a
# `vault:` section answers "Vault unusable" unless the check can log in where it runs;
# `uv run python tools/config_check.py --without-vault <file>` checks the rest.
#
# Every variable below is also in `docs/CONFIGURATION.md`, with what happens if you get it wrong.
# `make config-verify` reports a value this file sets that no container receives.

# ---- Where the published ports listen ----
# Loopback by default: this stack runs Postgres, Redis, Kafka and a dev-mode Vault with the
# credentials printed in this file, and Compose's plain `"5432:5432"` would put every one of them
# on every interface of your machine. `localhost` still reaches everything, which is what the
# Makefile and the docs use. Set 0.0.0.0 only if another machine genuinely has to reach this stack.
AIRA_BIND_HOST=127.0.0.1
# **And the IPv6 half.** Docker opens one socket per published entry, not one dual-stack socket,
# so setting only the line above leaves every IPv6 caller reaching a machine with nothing
# listening — which arrives as a connection **reset**, not a refusal, and therefore sends every
# diagnosis to the wrong end. A browser resolving `localhost` to `::1` is the ordinary case.
# Set both when something outside this machine has to reach the stack: `0.0.0.0` and `::`.
AIRA_BIND_HOST6=::1

# ---- PostgreSQL ----
POSTGRES_USER=aira
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=aira-local
POSTGRES_DB=aira

# ---- Keycloak DB role (provisioned by postgres/init) ----
KC_DB_PASSWORD=keycloak-local

# ---- Keycloak admin (dev only) ----
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN=admin
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD=admin

# ---- Vault (dev mode, local only) ----
VAULT_DEV_ROOT_TOKEN_ID=root

# ---- Redis: shared counters for rate limits and budget reservations (ADR-0008) ----
# The compose stack points the gateway at the in-network service. Override only to use an
# external Redis; leaving it empty disables the shared counters, which makes rate limits
# per-process and budget enforcement racy (both documented in docs/DEPLOYMENT.md).
AIRA_REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379/0
# Redis Sentinel instead of the URL: the sentinels as host:port,… and the leader's name. The
# passwords are secrets — Vault, not this file. USERNAME/PASSWORD/DB are read with Sentinel only:
# a single Redis takes its credentials inside the URL (redis://user:password@host:6379/0), which
# then belongs in Vault too.
#AIRA_REDIS_SENTINELS=redis-s1:26379,redis-s2:26379,redis-s3:26379
#AIRA_REDIS_SENTINEL_SERVICE=aira
#AIRA_REDIS_USERNAME=
#AIRA_REDIS_PASSWORD=…
#AIRA_REDIS_SENTINEL_PASSWORD=…
#AIRA_REDIS_DB=0

# --- Vertex AI in the EU (FRD-115) --------------------------------------------------------
# Google AI Studio (below) is the laptop path: one key, a global endpoint, and no residency
# statement. Vertex is the production one. Registered when PROJECT and a credential — CREDENTIALS,
# CREDENTIALS_FILE, METADATA_SERVER or API_KEY — are set; a model outside ALLOWED_REGIONS makes the gateway refuse to start.
# AIRA_VERTEX_PROJECT=my-gcp-project
# The service account's key as JSON — the content, one value, never a path to the file (a path is
# refused at start as not JSON). A secret: Vault in any real deployment.
# AIRA_VERTEX_CREDENTIALS={"client_email":"...","private_key":"..."}
# The same key as a file instead: put it at deploy/compose/secrets/vertex/key.json (ignored by
# git; readable by uid 10001 — `sudo chown 10001 key.json && chmod 400 key.json`), which the gateway
# sees at the path below. Read again whenever it changes, so a key written over it takes effect
# without a restart. Exclusive with AIRA_VERTEX_CREDENTIALS.
# AIRA_VERTEX_CREDENTIALS_FILE=/run/aira-secrets/vertex/key.json
# No Google key at all (workload identity federation): CREDENTIALS or CREDENTIALS_FILE then hold
# Google's external_account configuration — not a secret, `gcloud iam workload-identity-pools
# create-cred-config` writes it — and the gateway fetches its identity from your OIDC provider as a
# client of it: Keycloak's token endpoint, a client with service accounts enabled, its secret
# (Vault in any real deployment). docs/INTEGRATIONS.md has the steps on both sides.
# AIRA_VERTEX_FEDERATION_TOKEN_URL=https://sso.example.com/realms/aira/protocol/openid-connect/token
# AIRA_VERTEX_FEDERATION_CLIENT_ID=aira-gateway-gcp
# AIRA_VERTEX_FEDERATION_CLIENT_SECRET=
# On Google Cloud itself, the machine's own service account from the metadata server:
# AIRA_VERTEX_METADATA_SERVER=true
# The Google billing account whose prices the console fills when a model is catalogued, in
# AIRA_CURRENCY and at your contract price. The identity above needs Billing Account Viewer on it.
# AIRA_VERTEX_BILLING_ACCOUNT=012345-567890-ABCDEF
# The **other** credential this adapter takes (`FRD-115` FR-3a): an Agent Platform API key, sent
# as `x-goog-api-key` instead of an exchanged bearer token. With both set the service account wins.
# Claude on Vertex refuses an API key, so Anthropic's models need the service account.
# AIRA_VERTEX_API_KEY=
# Where requests may be processed — ONE list for every cloud, not one per vendor.
# Google says europe-west1 / eu, Azure says westeurope; the names differ, the policy
# does not. Empty means the EU regions of every supported cloud.
# AIRA_ALLOWED_REGIONS=eu,europe-west1,westeurope
# AIRA_VERTEX_MODELS=eu/google/gemini-2.5-pro,eu/anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5@20250929
# Agent Platform's managed open models — GLM (`zai-org`) and DeepSeek (`deepseek-ai`) — speak the
# OpenAI dialect at one endpoint and answer only at `global`, which names no region and guarantees
# none (`FRD-115` FR-2a). Using one is a decision about that model, so it is permitted by name
# rather than by adding `global` to the list above, which would permit it for every model:
# AIRA_ALLOWED_MODEL_REGIONS=global=glm-4.7-maas,deepseek-v3.2-maas

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local model endpoint (FRD-123, FRD-130)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `make showcase` starts Ollama in the `demo` profile and pulls these. The named-server form is
# what a self-hosted fleet actually looks like — several machines, each audited under its own name
# — so even a demo with one box uses it, rather than teaching a shape that does not scale.
#
#   name=url|chat models|embedding models|region
#
# The region is deliberately empty: a local box has no cloud region, and naming one would put a
# claim on every audit row that nobody can check (`FRD-115` FR-10).
AIRA_OPENAI_SERVERS=local=http://ollama:11434|qwen3:0.6b|all-minilm|
AIRA_DEMO_CHAT_MODEL=qwen3:0.6b
AIRA_DEMO_EMBED_MODEL=all-minilm

# Where requests may be processed. The local endpoint declares no region, so it is not constrained
# by this list; the cloud adapters are (`ADR-0012` §6 — one policy for every cloud).
# Google AI Studio (`AIRA_GOOGLE_API_KEY`, below) is the region `global`: that endpoint names no
# region and guarantees none, which is the whole difference from Vertex. It is checked like every
# other adapter's, so using it means adding `global` to this list — a decision in the file rather
# than one somebody remembers.
AIRA_ALLOWED_REGIONS=eu,europe-west1,europe-west3,europe-west4,westeurope

# ── Secrets from Vault (FRD-116) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Vault is used when VAULT_ADDR is set **and** VAULT_ROLE_ID (AppRole) or VAULT_TOKEN is. Without
# both halves there is "no Vault": every AIRA_* secret is read from this file instead — and an
# address on its own changes nothing and says nothing. `make vault-init` creates the path, the
# policy and the AppRole and prints the role id; `make vault-status` prints where the gateway's
# secrets came from (`/readyz` shows that to anyone locally, and elsewhere to an operator
# credential only).
#
# The address and the role id identify and do not authorise, so they belong here. The secret-id
# does authorise, and goes in a file with mode 0600 that is mounted into the containers.
# (VAULT_SECRET_ID, the variable, wins over the file when both are set.) Outside `local` a plain
# http:// address that is not loopback refuses to start, even before Vault is in use.
VAULT_ADDR=
VAULT_MOUNT=secret
VAULT_PATH=aira
VAULT_ROLE_ID=
VAULT_SECRET_ID_FILE=

# Local convenience only: the dev server's root token (`root`, above) instead of an AppRole, used
# only while VAULT_ROLE_ID is empty. Every use of it logs a warning, because a root token in a
# deployment is what this whole feature exists to prevent.
VAULT_TOKEN=

# Which Keycloak group confers which AIRA role (ADR-0017).
#
# Group membership is the ONLY source of a role: a realm role assigned directly to a person is not
# read and grants nothing. The two use-case roles are deliberately absent — administering or
# belonging to a use case is a grant on that use case (FRD-209), not a property of a person.
#
# Outside `local`, Management refuses to start when no group is named for global-admin: an
# installation nobody can administer cannot be repaired through its own console.
AIRA_ROLE_GROUPS=global-admin=/aira/global-admins;it-security=/aira/it-security;it-steuerung=/aira/it-steuerung

# How the relay and the consumer authenticate to Kafka.
#
# The config topics are applied straight into the gateway's read-model, which is where its
# authorization comes from — so anybody who can publish to them can grant themselves access to any
# use case, with no credential and no audit row. PLAINTEXT is the local default and both planes
# REFUSE TO START on it outside `local` (AIRA_DEMO_MODE waives that on the gateway alone).
AIRA_KAFKA_SECURITY_PROTOCOL=PLAINTEXT
# SASL_SSL in production, with a broker identity per service:
# AIRA_KAFKA_SECURITY_PROTOCOL=SASL_SSL
# AIRA_KAFKA_SASL_MECHANISM=SCRAM-SHA-512
# AIRA_KAFKA_SASL_USERNAME=aira-gateway
# AIRA_KAFKA_SASL_PASSWORD=... (from Vault)
# AIRA_KAFKA_SSL_CAFILE=/etc/ssl/private-ca.pem   # only for a private CA

# How many reverse proxies append to X-Forwarded-For in front of the gateway and Management. The
# source address is read that many entries FROM THE RIGHT; the left end is whatever the caller
# sent. Management reads it always — for its failed-authentication bound, and to believe a proxy's
# X-Forwarded-Proto — and takes the leftmost entry of a shorter chain, so make it exact. The
# gateway reads it only with AIRA_TRUST_FORWARDED_FOR on, and ignores a shorter chain.
AIRA_TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=1

# Authentication. `false` serves every route to anyone who can reach the port, with no credential
# and no use case — a laptop-demo switch and nothing else. The gateway refuses to start with it off
# unless AIRA_ENVIRONMENT=local, and AIRA_DEMO_MODE deliberately does not waive that.
# AIRA_AUTH_REQUIRED=true

# ---- Which images this stack runs (FRD-138) --------------------------------------------------
# Compose's own variables, read by `docker-compose.release.yml` and by no settings class — so no
# `config/*.example.yaml` names them. (`config_render.py` would not stop one that did: it refuses
# credentials, nothing else.)
#
# They are commented out on purpose. Only the release overlay reads them, and `RELEASE=` on a
# `make up*` target is what layers it on — and sets `AIRA_VERSION` from the same value, so through
# `make` a version written here is never the one that runs. The switch is on the command line,
# where it is visible in the shell history of whoever ran it:
#
#   make release-pull RELEASE=v0.1.1     # fetch the three images
#   make up-full RELEASE=v0.1.1          # run them on this repository's infrastructure
#   make up-integrated RELEASE=v0.1.1    # run them on infrastructure you provide
#
# AIRA_VERSION has **no default**: the composition is refused with a message naming it. `latest`
# exists in the registry and moves under a restart, so a stack pinned yesterday would not be
# pinned today and nothing would say so. If you do run a moving tag, set AIRA_PULL_POLICY=always.
# AIRA_VERSION=v0.1.1
# AIRA_IMAGE_REGISTRY=ghcr.io/inigoimago
# AIRA_PULL_POLICY=missing

# ---- Where this stack listens, and what it is called ----------------------------------------
# Two stacks on one machine collide on **both**: a fixed port and a fixed container name. Every
# published port is a variable below, and `AIRA_STACK` prefixes every container name — so a second
# copy needs a different name and whichever ports it must move, and nothing else.
#
#   AIRA_STACK=aira2 AIRA_PUBLISH_POSTGRES_PORT=5442 AIRA_PUBLISH_KEYCLOAK_PORT=8090 \
#     docker compose up -d
#
# (This example named `AIRA_POSTGRES_PORT` and `AIRA_KEYCLOAK_PORT` until 2026-09-02 —
# the first is the *setting* the paragraph below warns about, and the second is a name
# nothing in this repository defines. `test_one_owner_for_the_stack_addresses.py` records
# `AIRA_KEYCLOAK_PORT` as a past defect in the Makefile; the copy in this file outlived
# the fix, three lines above the paragraph explaining why it is wrong.)
#
# Note `COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME` too if you want separate volumes; sharing them is rarely what you
# want and never what you meant.
AIRA_STACK=aira

# The host a browser types to reach the console. Only the realm's redirect URIs read it, and only
# at import — see "REACHING THIS STACK FROM ANOTHER MACHINE" above. `localhost` and `127.0.0.1`
# stay valid whatever this says.
AIRA_CONSOLE_HOST=localhost
# `AIRA_PUBLISH_*` is deliberately its own prefix: `AIRA_POSTGRES_PORT` is already a **setting** —
# the port the gateway connects to — and reusing it here would have made "move the published port"
# silently mean "connect to a different port inside the network", where Postgres still listens on
# 5432. Two meanings, one name, and the failure looks like the database is down.
AIRA_PUBLISH_POSTGRES_PORT=5432
AIRA_PUBLISH_KEYCLOAK_PORT=8080
AIRA_PUBLISH_KEYCLOAK_HEALTH_PORT=9000
AIRA_PUBLISH_KAFKA_PORT=29092
AIRA_PUBLISH_SCHEMA_REGISTRY_PORT=8081
AIRA_PUBLISH_VAULT_PORT=8200
AIRA_PUBLISH_REDIS_PORT=6379
AIRA_PUBLISH_GRAFANA_PORT=3000
AIRA_PUBLISH_OTLP_GRPC_PORT=4317
AIRA_PUBLISH_OTLP_HTTP_PORT=4318
# The collector's own counters: what it accepted, and what it forwarded (`make otel-status`).
AIRA_PUBLISH_OTLP_METRICS_PORT=8889
# The KDC of the stand-in Windows domain (profile `windows-sso`, `FRD-140`), for the browser that
# plays one of its workstations in `make test-windows-sso`.
AIRA_PUBLISH_KDC_PORT=1088

# ---- What ARRIVES at the collector -----------------------------------------------------------
# Three questions, three answers, and they are easy to confuse:
#
#   what a service says it sent   AIRA_DEBUG_INTEGRATIONS=otel      (its own log)
#   what arrived at the collector AIRA_OTEL_DEBUG_VERBOSITY         (make otel-arrivals)
#   what the collector forwarded  make otel-status                  (its own counters)
#
# `basic` counts only · `normal` one line per span · `detailed` every attribute, event and link.
#AIRA_OTEL_DEBUG_VERBOSITY=normal
#
# The same arrivals as OTLP/JSON, to parse rather than read — all three signals, one document per
# line. `/dev/null` is off. `/dev/stdout` works for a look and mangles anything large (Docker's
# log driver cuts at 16 KiB); a file under deploy/compose/payload/ comes out whole.
#AIRA_OTEL_ARRIVED_FILE=/payload/arrived.json

# ================================================================================================
# TWO CHANNELS, AND THEY TAKE THE SAME SETTINGS
# ================================================================================================
# Everything below appears twice, once per channel, differing only in its prefix — AIRA_OTEL_BACKEND_
# for observability, AIRA_OTEL_FORWARD_ for delivery. That is FRD-620; before it this channel had
# four variables and no credential while the other had seventeen and four authenticators.
#
# Each channel has three fragment slots, answering three questions that do not depend on each other:
#
#   _CONFIG           whether, and where     (the on/off switch)
#   _PROTOCOL_CONFIG  how it is reached      (OTLP/HTTP, OTLP/gRPC, or a recipe)
#   _AUTH_CONFIG      who we say we are      (nothing, a header, or a recipe)
#
# **_ENDPOINT is the channel's default transport, and the other one has its own name.** The rule
# reads backwards between the two: observability defaults to gRPC, so its _ENDPOINT is host:port and
# _HTTP_ENDPOINT is the extra; delivery defaults to HTTP, so its _ENDPOINT is a URL and
# _GRPC_ENDPOINT is the extra. Those two names are the only difference between the families.
#
# **The encoding is reachable over HTTP only**, on either channel: OTLP/gRPC is protobuf by
# definition, so _ENCODING does not reach a gRPC leg.
#
# **A credential fragment selected without its values sends the name of the variable you forgot** —
# AIRA_OTEL_FORWARD_AUTHORIZATION-is-not-set — which `make otlp-inspector` shows and the far end
# quotes back. It used to stop the collector, taking Grafana with it.

# ---- Channel 1: observability, to your own trace backend ---------------------------------------
# Every span, metric and log. On by default; the bundled Grafana is only the default destination.
#
# Off entirely — keep the delivery channel, send the rest nowhere. Not AIRA_OTEL_ENABLED=false,
# which stops the applications exporting and so both channels. It outranks _PROTOCOL_CONFIG.
#AIRA_OTEL_BACKEND_CONFIG=/etc/otelcol-contrib/nobackend.yaml
#
# `host:port`, no scheme — OTLP/gRPC, this channel's default.
#AIRA_OTEL_BACKEND_ENDPOINT=tempo.internal:4317
# No TLS at all (h2c): right for a backend on this network, wrong across a boundary.
#AIRA_OTEL_BACKEND_PLAINTEXT=true
#
# OTLP/HTTP instead, which is what makes the encoding a choice: OTLP/gRPC is protobuf by definition.
# The collector appends /v1/traces, /v1/logs and /v1/metrics to the endpoint.
#AIRA_OTEL_BACKEND_PROTOCOL_CONFIG=/etc/otelcol-contrib/backend-http.yaml
#AIRA_OTEL_BACKEND_HTTP_ENDPOINT=https://tempo.internal:4318
# `json` or `proto`; any other value stops the collector at validation.
#AIRA_OTEL_BACKEND_ENCODING=json
# gzip | none | zstd | snappy, on either transport.
#AIRA_OTEL_BACKEND_COMPRESSION=gzip
#
# A credential in a header, under whichever name the backend wants; unset sends none. HTTP basic —
# Grafana Cloud's OTLP endpoint — is `Basic <base64 of user:password>` as the value.
#AIRA_OTEL_BACKEND_AUTH_CONFIG=/etc/otelcol-contrib/backend-auth-header.yaml
#AIRA_OTEL_BACKEND_AUTH_HEADER=x-api-key
#AIRA_OTEL_BACKEND_AUTHORIZATION=…
#
# A private CA, inside the collector (put the file in deploy/compose/otel/ca/). _INSECURE is not the
# answer to a missing root: it stops verifying every certificate on the leg.
#AIRA_OTEL_BACKEND_CA_FILE=/etc/otelcol-contrib/ca/your-ca.crt
#AIRA_OTEL_BACKEND_INSECURE=false
# Mutual TLS, where the backend authenticates *us* by certificate.
#AIRA_OTEL_BACKEND_CLIENT_CERT_FILE=
#AIRA_OTEL_BACKEND_CLIENT_KEY_FILE=
# The collector's own defaults.
#AIRA_OTEL_BACKEND_BATCH_SECONDS=200ms
#AIRA_OTEL_BACKEND_BATCH_SIZE=8192
#AIRA_OTEL_BACKEND_CONSUMERS=10
#AIRA_OTEL_BACKEND_QUEUE=1000
#AIRA_OTEL_BACKEND_RETRY_INITIAL=5s
#
# ---- Channel 2: delivery — a second destination beside it --------------------------------------
# Your SIEM, a datalake, another collector. Both are needed: the first merges the forwarding
# fragment, the second says where. Fan-out, not replacement — Grafana keeps working. What arrives is
# OTLP/JSON; docs/INTEGRATIONS.md §6 says what that shape is before you plan a parser around it.
#AIRA_OTEL_FORWARD_CONFIG=/etc/otelcol-contrib/forward.yaml
#AIRA_OTEL_FORWARD_ENDPOINT=https://siem.internal:4318
#
# OTLP/gRPC instead: one address, no scheme, no JSON.
#AIRA_OTEL_FORWARD_PROTOCOL_CONFIG=/etc/otelcol-contrib/forward-grpc.yaml
#AIRA_OTEL_FORWARD_GRPC_ENDPOINT=siem.internal:4317
# No TLS at all (h2c) — not _INSECURE, which keeps the encryption and skips the check.
#AIRA_OTEL_FORWARD_GRPC_PLAINTEXT=false
#
# `json` or `proto`. The specification makes protobuf required and JSON optional, so a receiver may
# refuse JSON. Any other value stops the collector at validation.
#AIRA_OTEL_FORWARD_ENCODING=json
# gzip | none | zstd | snappy. `none` is for a receiver that does not unwrap gzip. Plain,
# uncompressed JSON is ENCODING=json with COMPRESSION=none; there is no encoding called `none`.
#AIRA_OTEL_FORWARD_COMPRESSION=gzip
#
# A credential in a header, under whichever name the receiver wants — x-api-key, DD-API-KEY, …
# HTTP basic is `Basic <base64 of user:password>`. Selected without a value, it sends the name of
# the variable you forgot.
#AIRA_OTEL_FORWARD_AUTH_CONFIG=/etc/otelcol-contrib/forward-auth-header.yaml
#AIRA_OTEL_FORWARD_AUTH_HEADER=authorization
#AIRA_OTEL_FORWARD_AUTHORIZATION=Bearer …
#
# A private CA (deploy/compose/otel/ca/), and mutual TLS.
#AIRA_OTEL_FORWARD_CA_FILE=/etc/otelcol-contrib/ca/your-ca.crt
#AIRA_OTEL_FORWARD_INSECURE=false
#AIRA_OTEL_FORWARD_CLIENT_CERT_FILE=
#AIRA_OTEL_FORWARD_CLIENT_KEY_FILE=
#
# Polite rather than fast — one sender, a ten-second window, a 5 s first retry — because a 429 is
# retryable and the collector's own defaults tighten that loop.
#AIRA_OTEL_FORWARD_BATCH_SECONDS=10s
#AIRA_OTEL_FORWARD_BATCH_SIZE=8192
#AIRA_OTEL_FORWARD_CONSUMERS=1
#AIRA_OTEL_FORWARD_QUEUE=5000
#AIRA_OTEL_FORWARD_RETRY_INITIAL=5s
#
# ---- Beyond the core: recipes (ADR-0024) -------------------------------------------------------
# Splunk HEC, OAuth2 client credentials and Azure Monitor are fragments in
# deploy/compose/otel/recipes/, selected through _PROTOCOL_CONFIG or _AUTH_CONFIG above. Their own
# values go in deploy/compose/otel/custom/collector.env, not here; recipes/README.md lists them.
#AIRA_OTEL_FORWARD_PROTOCOL_CONFIG=/etc/otelcol-contrib/recipes/forward-splunk-hec.yaml
#
# `host.docker.internal` is already mapped, for a receiver port-forwarded to this machine. For one
# elsewhere, a hostname or an IP in the endpoint above is enough — the container resolves both.
#
# ---- And a receiver to try it against, before you have one -------------------------------------
# `make otlp-inspector` starts an OTLP receiver with a page in front of it: what **leaves** on the
# forwarding leg, which is not what `make otel-arrivals` shows (that is what *arrived*, before the
# filter). Point the endpoint above at http://otlp-inspector:4318 and look.
#
# A debugging tool — in memory, capped, unauthenticated, and it holds attribution. A developer's
# machine, and nowhere else.
#AIRA_PUBLISH_OTLP_INSPECTOR_PORT=4319
#AIRA_OTLP_INSPECTOR_KEEP=200
#
# **Two addresses.** `otlp-inspector:4318` is a Docker name and the container's port — it exists
# only inside this stack's network, so it is what a collector on THIS machine uses. From anywhere
# else, including a collector on another machine, it is this host on the published port above, and
# the same port serves the page and /v1/{traces,logs,metrics}:
#
#   AIRA_OTEL_FORWARD_ENDPOINT=http://otlp-inspector:4318   collector here
#   AIRA_OTEL_FORWARD_ENDPOINT=http://<this-host>:4319      collector elsewhere
#
# Reaching it from elsewhere needs AIRA_BIND_HOST and AIRA_BIND_HOST6 set — see the section at the
# top of this file.
AIRA_PUBLISH_OLLAMA_PORT=11434
AIRA_PUBLISH_GATEWAY_PORT=8001
AIRA_PUBLISH_MANAGEMENT_PORT=8002
AIRA_PUBLISH_FRONTEND_PORT=4200

# Whether the **applications** export traces, metrics and logs at all.
#
# Off by default, and the collector and Grafana run either way — so with this unset the whole
# observability stack is healthy, answers on its port, and shows an empty Explore view that looks
# exactly like a broken one. Turn it on and the two planes appear in Tempo under `aira-gateway`
# and `aira-management`; a request's own id comes back on the `x-trace-id` response header, which
# is the fastest way to find one (search takes about a minute to index, an id resolves at once).
#
# What travels: attribution, model, outcome, tokens, cost, source address — never a prompt or a
# response. See `docs/CONFIGURATION.md`.
AIRA_OTEL_ENABLED=false

# ==============================================================================================
# The rest of the contract.
#
# Every remaining `AIRA_*` a settings class accepts. Commented out: an uncommented line here
# **is** the value, and a file that sets ninety of them is a file where nobody can see which six
# were decisions. Uncomment what you mean to change.
#
# The value shown is the code's default, **except** where that default is empty or is a
# `localhost` address that only the from-source shape uses — there it is an example or the
# in-network name, and the line says so. `docs/CONFIGURATION.md` carries the defaults as a table
# dumped from the settings classes rather than remembered, which is the copy to trust.
# ==============================================================================================

# ---- What this installation is, and how loud ------------------------------------------------
# `local` waives the hardening checks; on anything else — no other value is special, `demo`
# included — a dev secret, AIRA_DEBUG=true, a wildcard ALLOWED_HOSTS, PLAINTEXT Kafka, OIDC without
# an audience or AUTH_REQUIRED=false all refuse to boot (ADR-0015). Only `local` (or
# AIRA_DEMO_MODE) has the gateway serve /docs, /redoc and /openapi.json, register the mock
# upstream and show its whole /readyz diagnosis to anyone.
#AIRA_ENVIRONMENT=local
# Seeds the demo API key and lets `seed_demo` run, and waives six named checks on the gateway
# (`WAIVED_BY_A_DEMO`: the Postgres password, the OIDC audience, a plain-http issuer or JWKS URI,
# PLAINTEXT Kafka, and a plain-http VAULT_ADDR) — none on Management, which still refuses outside
# `local`. It does **not** waive AIRA_AUTH_REQUIRED or AIRA_REQUIRE_USE_CASE: a demo with those off
# would be an open port serving models to anyone who found it.
#AIRA_DEMO_MODE=false
#AIRA_LOG_LEVEL=INFO
# `false` gives human-readable lines for a terminal — what the dev shape wants.
#AIRA_LOG_JSON=true
# The OTel `service.name` (and the gateway's `/version-info` service). The two planes default
# differently (`aira-gateway` / `aira-management`); setting it here hands both containers the one
# value and names them the same, which is rarely what you want.
#AIRA_APP_NAME=aira-gateway
# One installation, one currency. Labels every figure and every CSV column; no conversion happens.
#AIRA_CURRENCY=EUR

# ---- One line per call to an external system (FRD-617) ---------------------------------------
# For the days when something is being integrated and "did we send it, and did it arrive" has no
# answer anywhere. Empty is off, and off is what a working installation runs.
#   otel · kafka · auth · vault · redis · postgres — or `all`
# A name this build does not know refuses the process at start-up rather than watching nothing.
# No second switch: the lines are INFO/WARNING, so you do not also need AIRA_LOG_LEVEL=DEBUG.
# On a busy gateway `redis` is the only per-request system — `kafka,auth,vault,otel` is the quiet
# set. Every line carries the trace_id of the request that caused it.
#AIRA_DEBUG_INTEGRATIONS=

# The OTLP batch itself, as OTLP/JSON, on stdout — for handing a real payload to whoever has to
# parse it. A number: how many items of each batch to render. 0 is off.
#
# **Not what goes over this leg.** The applications post `application/x-protobuf` and cannot be
# made to post JSON; this is the protobuf-JSON *mapping* of the same content. For the exact bytes
# your endpoint receives, use the collector: `AIRA_OTEL_ARRIVED_FILE`, above.
#
# Expensive and loud — it renders on the exporter's thread, on every export — and it carries span
# attributes: subject, use case, model, source address. No prompt or response ever reaches a span.
#AIRA_DEBUG_OTEL_PAYLOAD=3

# ---- Databases -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# **Two different databases**: the gateway's `aira_gateway` and Management's `aira_mgmt`, on the
# same server. The compose stack sets host/user/password per container; override only to point at
# a Postgres of your own.
#AIRA_POSTGRES_HOST=postgres     # the in-network name; default `localhost` is the
                                 # from-source shape, where the stack's port is published
#AIRA_POSTGRES_PORT=5432
#AIRA_POSTGRES_USER=aira
#AIRA_POSTGRES_PASSWORD=…            # belongs in Vault outside `local`
#AIRA_POSTGRES_DB=aira_gateway
# The bus both planes carry configuration on. In-network name for the stack; a broker list of
# your own for an integrated deployment.
#AIRA_KAFKA_BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS=kafka:9092   # in-network; default `localhost:29092`
# The stage in every topic name and in the gateway's consumer group, on a cluster several
# stages share: `t` gives `aira.t.usecases` and `aira-gateway.t`. Both planes need the same one.
#AIRA_KAFKA_STAGE=

# ---- Keycloak / OIDC (FRD-101, FRD-134) ------------------------------------------------------
# Whether the gateway verifies a bearer token at all. Off, only API keys authenticate.
#AIRA_OIDC_ENABLED=true          # this stack's default; the settings class's own is false
# The issuer as a **browser** reaches it: compared literally with `iss`, and written into the
# console's runtime-config.js. This stack's default is http://localhost:8080/realms/aira.
#AIRA_OIDC_ISSUER=http://keycloak:8080/realms/aira   # an example; the settings default is empty
# **Set this outside local.** Without an audience, any token the realm issued is accepted here —
# including one minted for an unrelated client. Both planes refuse to start without it when
# AIRA_ENVIRONMENT is not `local` (the gateway while OIDC is on).
#AIRA_OIDC_AUDIENCE=aira-gateway           # an example; the default is empty
# Where the containers fetch the realm's keys. Derived from the issuer, except here: this stack
# defaults it to the in-network http://keycloak:8080/…, because a container cannot reach the
# browser's `localhost`. `make up-integrated` requires it.
#AIRA_OIDC_JWKS_URI=
# Several realms at once, for a migration: `issuer|audience|jwks_uri` entries, `;`-separated.
# A token is routed by its own `iss` and then verified for real against that entry. Gateway only:
# Management and the console read the single issuer above.
#AIRA_OIDC_ISSUERS=
# How far the issuer's clock may run AHEAD of this host. Accepting it extends nobody's access —
# a verifier one second behind its issuer otherwise refuses every freshly minted token as
# not-yet-valid, answered as 401 and indistinguishable from a wrong secret (FRD-134).
#AIRA_OIDC_CLOCK_SKEW_SECONDS=60.0
# How long PAST expiry a token is still accepted. **Zero deliberately** — this is the half that
# extends a credential's life beyond what the issuer granted. Max 300 either way; both planes
# read both.
#AIRA_OIDC_EXPIRY_LEEWAY_SECONDS=0.0
# A read-only Keycloak service account (`view-users`, `query-groups`) so the console can search
# people who have never signed in (FRD-209). BOTH are needed: without either, the search falls
# back to what Management already knows and says so. The secret belongs in Vault.
# Binding a role to a group (FRD-614) is refused while nobody can check the group, so custom roles
# need the client. The compose stack defaults to the development realm's `aira-directory`.
#AIRA_DIRECTORY_CLIENT_ID=aira-directory
#AIRA_DIRECTORY_CLIENT_SECRET=…
# Where Management reaches Keycloak's admin API, when that is not the server in AIRA_OIDC_ISSUER
# (inside a container it rarely is). The compose stack defaults to http://keycloak:8080.
#AIRA_DIRECTORY_URL=http://keycloak:8080

# ---- Management: the model reference ------------------------------------------------------------
# What "+ Add a model" fills a new entry from: limits, capabilities, how a model reasons and its
# list price, from a public list, for you to check. Empty uses the copy this release ships; set the
# list's address to read today's (Management asks it at most once a day).
#AIRA_MODEL_REFERENCE_URL=https://models.dev/api.json
# Units of AIRA_CURRENCY per US dollar, for the reference's list prices where the billing account
# has none (e.g. 0.92). Empty: a dollar price is shown beside the field, not filled.
#AIRA_MODEL_REFERENCE_EXCHANGE_RATE=0.92

# ---- Management: the privacy notice (FRD-625) --------------------------------------------------
# Every console user reads and acknowledges it. The controller (name, address, contact) is printed
# as Art. 13(1)(a) DSGVO requires, and Management **refuses to boot without it** outside `local`.
#AIRA_PRIVACY_CONTROLLER=Example GmbH, Example Street 1, 12345 Example, privacy@example.com
# The data protection officer's contact; empty prints that none is recorded.
#AIRA_PRIVACY_DPO_CONTACT=dpo@example.com
# The works agreement (Betriebsvereinbarung) governing this system; empty prints that none is.
#AIRA_PRIVACY_WORKS_AGREEMENT=BV 2026-04 KI-Gateway
# When the window opens: `once` per edition, `monthly` (per edition and at the first start of each
# month), or `always` — every start of the console, for whoever is reviewing the notice.
#AIRA_PRIVACY_NOTICE_MODE=monthly
# The language for a browser that asks for none the notice exists in (`de`, `en`).
#AIRA_PRIVACY_DEFAULT_LANGUAGE=de

# ---- Management: Django ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signs sessions and CSRF tokens. The default is a well-known development value and Management
# refuses to boot on it outside `local`. Vault, not this file.
#AIRA_SECRET_KEY=…
# Django's DEBUG renders tracebacks containing settings and environment. Honoured only in `local`
# and **refused** anywhere else — the default `true` included, so a deployment sets it false.
#AIRA_DEBUG=true
# The names this installation is served under. `*` is refused outside `local`.
#AIRA_ALLOWED_HOSTS=localhost,127.0.0.1    # default `*`, which is refused outside `local`
# The first is DRF's per-user throttle. The second is not a DRF throttle: it bounds failed
# authentications per source address, counting refusals only, so no working integration can trip
# it — `0/minute` switches it off, and a bare `0` refuses to boot. Both count per process.
#AIRA_THROTTLE_USER=600/minute
#AIRA_THROTTLE_AUTH_FAILURES=60/minute

# ---- Gateway: what it enforces ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Each of these is ON by default. Turning one off is a governance decision, not a tuning knob.
# With budgets off, both budget screens in the console say so (`FRD-610` §3.3) — the figures stay
# true, and what would otherwise be false is the impression that a limit is in force.
#AIRA_ENFORCE_BUDGETS=true
#AIRA_ENFORCE_RATE_LIMITS=true
#AIRA_ENFORCE_SUSPENSIONS=true
# The fourth of the family, and the narrowest: a model with **no price on file** cannot be counted
# against a *cost* budget, so it would spend without bound (`FRD-610` §3.2). On, such a model is
# refused where a cost limit binds the request — a dispatch condition, so a chain tries the next
# candidate and a priced fallback still serves. `false` keeps the traffic flowing while the prices
# are filled in, knowing the limit is not a limit. Token and request limits are unaffected.
#AIRA_ENFORCE_PRICED_MODELS=true
# A stream whose caller left before the vendor said what it used is otherwise booked as nothing.
# On, the vendor's own free token count is asked for the request and the answer received, that
# floor is booked and the row marked `counted` (`FRD-128` FR-6). `false` sends nothing to the
# vendor a second time — which the privacy notice states — and such a stream stays unknown.
#AIRA_COUNT_CANCELLED_STREAMS=true
# Detection itself. Off means no evaluation at all; rules already authored stay authored.
#AIRA_DETECT_ANOMALIES=true
#AIRA_ANOMALY_INTERVAL_SECONDS=60.0
# Whether an authenticated caller must name a use case. Off, a request is charged to no budget,
# bounded by no limit, outside the model release, with an audit row naming nobody — refused
# anywhere but `local`, AIRA_DEMO_MODE included.
#AIRA_REQUIRE_USE_CASE=true
# Failed authentications one source address may make per minute. Counts refusals only; 0 disables.
#AIRA_MAX_AUTH_FAILURES_PER_MINUTE=60
# Assumed output length when the caller bounds none, used to reserve budget before the real usage
# is known. Reconciled the moment the response arrives; erring high is the safe direction.
#AIRA_BUDGET_ESTIMATE_OUTPUT_TOKENS=1024

# ---- Gateway: what it stores, and for how long (FRD-103, FRD-404) ----------------------------
# Management reads these four as well: the privacy notice states them (FRD-625).
# Prompts and responses on the audit row. Off, only metadata is kept — and the evidence half of
# this product goes with it.
#AIRA_STORE_PAYLOADS=true
# Strict data mode: nobody but Global Administrator and IT Security sees a person — content,
# figures per person, the name and key on a request, the person a finding names — and nobody sees
# latency per person. Off, each use case's own content setting decides again. Both planes.
#AIRA_STRICT_DATA_MODE=true
# The key telemetry and logs pseudonymise a person's name with under strict data mode — one person,
# one pseudonym, in both planes. A credential: from Vault. Unset, each process draws its own, so no
# name leaks but a SIEM cannot follow one caller across processes and restarts.
#AIRA_PSEUDONYM_SECRET=…
# Retention for traffic that carries no use case. Use-case traffic follows its own use case. At
# least 1: the sweep reads 0 or less as one day, while the privacy notice prints it as no limit.
#AIRA_DEFAULT_RETENTION_DAYS=7
# Delete whole audit rows older than this. **0 keeps them forever**, which is the historical
# behaviour and what the cost reporting reads. Opt in deliberately.
#AIRA_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS=0
# Requests buffered for the off-path audit writer. 0 writes every record on the request path.
#AIRA_LOG_QUEUE_SIZE=512
# Extra redaction patterns for stored payloads, `;`- or newline-separated regexes. **Additive**:
# the built-in credential shapes always apply. An invalid or catastrophically backtracking
# pattern stops the gateway rather than silently matching nothing.
#AIRA_REDACT_PATTERNS=

# ---- Gateway: request hardening (ADR-0007) ---------------------------------------------------
# Honour X-Forwarded-For for the recorded source IP. Only behind a proxy you control — otherwise
# callers can forge the audit trail.
#AIRA_TRUST_FORWARDED_FOR=false
#AIRA_TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=1
# Hard ceiling on an accepted request body (8 MiB).
#AIRA_MAX_REQUEST_BYTES=8388608
# Bounds on a caller-supplied response schema, and on embedding batches. Each refuses by name
# rather than dying somewhere downstream.
#AIRA_MAX_RESPONSE_SCHEMA_BYTES=32768
#AIRA_MAX_RESPONSE_SCHEMA_DEPTH=8
#AIRA_MAX_RESPONSE_SCHEMA_PROPERTIES=256
#AIRA_MAX_EMBEDDING_BATCH=256
#AIRA_MAX_EMBEDDING_CHARS=1000000
# Browsers that may call the gateway directly. Empty means none; `AIRA_CORS_ORIGINS=` clears it.
#AIRA_CORS_ORIGINS=
#AIRA_CORS_ALLOW_CREDENTIALS=false

# ---- API keys ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A key is always bounded at both ends. Asking for more than the maximum is refused by name.
# Shared by both planes: Management issues keys and the gateway's CLI mints the break-glass one.
#AIRA_API_KEY_DEFAULT_DAYS=30
#AIRA_API_KEY_MAX_DAYS=180

# ---- Upstream timeouts and ceilings ------------------------------------------------------------
#AIRA_VERTEX_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=120.0
#AIRA_FOUNDRY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=120.0
# Long on purpose: a scaled-to-zero local model pays a cold start on its first call. The allowance
# for every OpenAI-compatible server, AIRA_OPENAI_SERVERS entries included.
#AIRA_OLLAMA_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=300.0
# Anthropic on Vertex requires a token bound on every request and the dialect has no "unbounded";
# this is what is sent when the caller names none.
#AIRA_VERTEX_DEFAULT_MAX_TOKENS=4096
# Claude's tools sent strict on Vertex (the provider guarantees the arguments). Needs the
# organisation policy constraints/vertexai.allowedPartnerModelFeatures to allow structured_outputs,
# or every request carrying a tool is refused.
#AIRA_VERTEX_PARTNER_STRICT_TOOLS=false

# ---- Google AI Studio (the laptop path) --------------------------------------------------------
# One key, a global endpoint, no residency statement. The endpoint's region is `global`, so using
# it means adding `global` to AIRA_ALLOWED_REGIONS — a decision in the file, not one somebody
# remembers. Vertex (above) is the production path.
#AIRA_GOOGLE_API_KEY=
#AIRA_GEMINI_MODELS=gemini-2.0-flash,gemini-1.5-flash   # examples; the default is empty
# Keep the /v1beta: the adapter appends /models/<model>:<method> to it.
#AIRA_GEMINI_BASE_URL=https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta

# ---- Azure AI Foundry (FRD-120) -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Registered when an endpoint and deployments are set; an endpoint without a key refuses to start.
# Deployments are `model=deployment|region[|embed]` entries separated by `;`, the region required.
#AIRA_FOUNDRY_ENDPOINT=https://my-resource.services.ai.azure.com   # example; default empty
#AIRA_FOUNDRY_API_KEY=
#AIRA_FOUNDRY_API_VERSION=2024-10-21
#AIRA_FOUNDRY_DEPLOYMENTS=gpt-4o=my-gpt4o|westeurope               # example; default empty

# ---- A self-hosted model, named individually (FRD-123) ------------------------------------------
# The single-endpoint form. `AIRA_OPENAI_SERVERS` above is the shape a fleet actually takes and is
# what the stack uses; this is here because a single box is the common first step.
#AIRA_OLLAMA_URL=http://ollama:11434        # examples; all three default to empty
#AIRA_OLLAMA_MODELS=qwen3:0.6b
#AIRA_OLLAMA_EMBEDDING_MODELS=all-minilm
# Recorded on every audit row. A self-hosted model is the strongest residency story available, and
# one that nothing records is a claim rather than evidence. Empty declares none, which is honest
# for a laptop and is why it is not defaulted.
#AIRA_OLLAMA_REGION=

# ---- Telemetry destination ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where OTLP goes. The switch itself is AIRA_OTEL_ENABLED, above.
#AIRA_OTEL_ENDPOINT=http://otel-collector:4318   # in-network; default `http://localhost:4318`
# 1.0 = every trace, decided by this installation and never by a caller's `traceparent` flag.
# Lowering it leaves the audit trail (`request_logs`) whole, and **thins the delivery channel to a
# SIEM** by the same share: its access records are spans.
#AIRA_OTEL_SAMPLE_RATIO=1.0

# ---- The KIRA compatibility surface (FRD-107) ----------------------------------------------------
# Sent as written, unchecked, in the `Sunset` header of every KIRA response — RFC 8594 asks for an
# HTTP-date, e.g. `Sat, 31 Oct 2026 23:59:59 GMT`. Empty sends no `Sunset`. `Deprecation: true` is
# sent either way: the surface says from day one that it is transitional.
#AIRA_KIRA_SUNSET=

# ---- Not settable through this file --------------------------------------------------------------
# AIRA_TEST_DATABASE          an in-memory SQLite; set by the test harness, never by a deployment.
#                             Management refuses it outside `local`; the gateway does not, and
#                             would start on an empty database that loses everything on restart.
# AIRA_GIT_COMMIT / _BRANCH / AIRA_BUILD_NUMBER / AIRA_BUILD_TIME
#                             build metadata, served by the gateway's /version-info. Nothing stamps
#                             it yet — the Dockerfiles and CI pass no build argument — so it reports
#                             nulls. Not offered here: a value passed at run time would let a
#                             container claim a commit it was not built from.

